---
import TrainingLayout from "../../layouts/TrainingLayout.astro";
import Snippet from "../../components/training/Snippet.astro";
import LiveDemo from "../../components/training/LiveDemo.astro";
import ApiLink from "../../components/training/ApiLink.astro";
import ChapterLink from "../../components/training/ChapterLink.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/training/Callout.astro";
---
<TrainingLayout slug="tree-table">

  <h2 id="data">Hierarchical data</h2>
  <Callout type="version" title="Flat data? Row grouping (since 1.1.94)">
    <p>This chapter starts from data that is already nested. If your rows are flat and you only want them grouped by
      columns with totals per group, let the table build the tree: <ChapterLink slug="row-grouping" />.</p>
  </Callout>
  <p>5,000 orders, grouped by country → customer → order. Every node has its own totals and a unique id:</p>
  <Snippet file="demos/tree-table.ts" region="data" title="src/model/order-tree.ts" />

  <h2 id="tree-rows">From objects to tree rows</h2>
  <p>
    <ApiLink name="TreeFactory.buildTreeRows" /> wraps every object into a <ApiLink name="TreeRow" /> (with
    <code>expanded</code> state and children). <ApiLink name="TableFactory.createTableModel" /> recognises tree rows and creates an
    <ApiLink name="AreaModelTree" /> – the first column gets the arrows.
  </p>
  <Snippet file="demos/tree-table.ts" region="model" />
  <ul>
    <li><code>childrenProperty</code> – where the children are in your objects.</li>
    <li><code>expanded: false</code> – start collapsed (default: everything open). Also a function:
      <code>(row, deep) =&gt; deep === 0</code> opens only the first level.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="checkboxes">Checkboxes</h2>
  <Snippet file="demos/tree-table.ts" region="table" />
  <p>
    <ApiLink name="TableOptionsIf.showCheckboxWihoutExtraColumn" /> (sic) puts a checkbox into the tree column, right after the arrow.
    <ApiLink name="EventListenerIf.onCheckboxChanged" /> gets all checked rows; checking a parent checks its children.
  </p>
  <LiveDemo name="tree-table" title="Orders as a tree" height={440}
            caption="Click ▸ to expand, Alt+click in the first column works too. The icon top left expands or collapses everything." />

  <Callout type="tip" title="Renderers work in trees">
    <p>Total and status use the renderers of <ChapterLink slug="cell-renderers" />. Parent rows have no status – the badge renderer
      simply draws nothing for empty values.</p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout type="version">
    <p><code>expanded</code> in <code>buildTreeRows</code> since 1.1.90. Since 1.1.89 empty parent cells show nothing instead of
      “undefined”, and two quick clicks on different arrows are no longer swallowed as a double-click.</p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout type="note" title="Filter a tree">
    <p>The filter function gets the <ApiLink name="TreeRowIf" /> (use <code>row.data</code>) and checks every node – also below
      collapsed nodes. Ancestors of a match stay visible, and the filter stays active while you expand, collapse or sort
      (since 1.1.91).</p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout type="note" title="Remember what was open">
    <p>With <code>autoRestoreOptions</code> and <code>getRowId</code> the table remembers expanded nodes across reloads –
      <ChapterLink slug="persist-state" />.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="showcases">Showcases</h2>
  <p><a href="/showcase/treetable/run/">Tree table</a> and <a href="/showcase/treetablecheckbox/run/">Tree table with checkboxes</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="next">Next</h2>
  <p>Part 7: the table becomes editable – <ChapterLink slug="inline-editing" />.</p>
</TrainingLayout>
